Since obediently binge-watching all eight episodes of Marvel's ensemble mini-series The Defenders on Friday, I've been trying to figure out why it felt so hollow. In Matt Murdock (Charlie Cox), Jessica Jones (Krysten Ritter) and Luke Cage (Mike Colter), this corner of the Marvel universe has three genuinely compelling and three-dimensional characters—their relatively modest superpowers allow more room for emotional nuance. But their first team-up makes the baffling decision early on to focus a lot of energy on Danny Rand (Finn Jones), aka Iron Fist, the franchise's newest and least successful character.
If you're wondering how much of the Marvel/Netflix TV universe you need to have seen in order to make sense of The Defenders, the short answer is just the shows about the white guys. Only Daredevil season two and Iron Fist season one have any direct bearing on The Defenders, with Jessica Jones and Luke Cage reduced essentially to quippy, expository sidekick roles. Sure, Jessica and Luke's solo shows are both getting second seasons, and somebody has to get the short straw in a team-up, but the show's insistent doubling down on Danny—barely five months on from his own show's disastrous debut—only emphasizes the character's flimsiness. That imbalance at the core of The Defenders' narrative means that, despite a whole bunch of entertaining individual set pieces and character beats, the show ultimately feels like a gap-filler that never really gets off the ground.
The show inauspiciously opens on a slow-mo Iron Fist fight scene, as Danny hunts down members of the shadowy ancient organization, The Hand, which is trying to destroy New York City because it's supervillain tradition at this point. Danny also has a lot of feelings and trauma and guilt which we get to hear about ad nauseam, as the show gradually dovetails his storyline with those of Jessica, Matt, and Luke—all of whom are coming off their own respective show storylines more determined than ever to work alone. There's a meta level on which these people are all aware that they have their own shows to front and have zero interest in being part of an ensemble.
Netflix
Forcing this weirdo group of dysfunctional loners to work together is where The Defenders has the most fun, and though it takes a few episodes to get them into the same room, it's worth the wait. The sparky dynamic between Cox's somber Matt and Ritter's prickly Jessica is an unexpected highlight, while Danny becomes infinitely more likable when he's being sardonically side-eyed by his Defender pals. The show takes satisfaction in having Danny proudly announce his own ridiculous credentials–"I am the Immortal Iron Fist"–to the bemused, unimpressed other three, drawing a clear distinction between Danny's pomposity and the trio's shared reluctance to even call themselves heroes.
The show's third episode, possibly its best overall, pivots on a scene in which Danny, a white man born into enormous wealth who loves to talk about how he earned his powers, is taken to task by Luke, a working class black man recently released from the jail where he was wrongfully imprisoned. A heated debate between the two is sparked after Luke discovers Danny beating the hell out of a black kid who's indirectly working for The Hand. To Danny, anyone working for the enemy is fair game, but to Luke, this is a poor kid from his block who's just trying to make a living. "I know privilege when I see it," Luke tells Danny. "You may think you earned your strength, but you had power before the day you were born." While the scene feels like a very pointed response to Iron Fist's critics, who called out the show for perpetuating the tired narrative of a white Chosen One granted superpowers by a mystical Asian realm, it's still a smart, convincing scene that's fully grounded in the worlds both these characters come from.
Netflix
