ANN ARBOR, MI - The demolition of the South Main Market, a popular shopping center in Ann Arbor that housed a collection of local businesses, has begun.
As of Friday morning, Dec. 16, crews already had demolished one building and were beginning work on the next.
The car wash next to the market also is coming down, making way for a nearly 290,000-square-foot apartment development with some retail space.
The brick facade of the old Ann Arbor Buggy Company building at the corner of Main and Mosley is being preserved and incorporated into the design of the new building, known as The Residences at 615 South Main.
By the Pound, a bulk food store that operated at the South Main Market, relocated to a new space at 1952 S. Industrial Highway on Dec. 1.
Owner Glenn Bourland said on Friday he's hoping to eventually move back to Main Street into one of the new retail spaces included in the development if there's adequate customer parking, but that's still a question.
He said he's also considering the possibility of having two stores because he really likes his new space.
"I really like this location. It's great," he said.
Other businesses forced to move out of the South Main Market, including San Fu Oriental Cuisine and Back Alley Gourmet, are looking for new locations. Ann Arbor Shoe Repair has moved to the Briarwood Mall.
The Collegiate Development Group plans to construct a 229-unit, six-story building on the east side of Main Street, just north of Mosley Street, similar in nature to the 618 South Main apartments across the street. The City Council voted unanimously to approve the plans in July.
The new building will include a mix of micro studios and larger studios, as well as one-, two-, three-, four- and five-bedroom apartments.
It's expected there will be 588 bedrooms total. The target population is young professionals, empty nesters and graduate students.
The plans also include an onsite leasing center, clubhouse, fitness center, pool courtyard and ground-floor retail space.
