Turns out you don't have to become "Bridezilla" when you know a top banana.
Chicago businesswoman Mellody Hobson didn't have to throw temper tantrums to get the wedding reception of her dreams. She is a close friend and campaign donor of Mayor Rahm Emanuel's.
As the Tribune reported this week, the Chicago Park District gave special attention to Hobson's Chicago celebration of her California wedding. The festivities took place on Promontory Point, a gorgeous little park that's also a popular wedding and corporate reception destination on the lakefront, not far from the Museum of Science and Industry.
Park District officials waived the rules on choice of caterer, bathroom usage and hours. And wouldn't you know it? A walkway under Lake Shore Drive used to access Promontory Point got a fresh coat of paint and an asphalt patch job.
At first, the Park District denied the sprucing-up was tied to the reception, but come on.
Hobson's wedding planner eventually paid for the cost of the fix-ups — an acknowledgment that Chicago taxpayers shouldn't be coughing up the resources to beautify a very private wedding reception at a public park.
But even that isn't the issue. Most Chicagoans probably don't care that a superstar party — Hobson married "Star Wars" and "Indiana Jones" creator George Lucas — got extra attention from the city. Nobody wants Chicago looking shabby for Hollywood.
The issue is that the Park District should be more accommodating, not less.
Hobson and Lucas paid nearly $100,000 to rent the space, $23,000 to reserve nearby parking lots and more than $7,000 for the walkway work. In exchange for their money, they got flexibility. They used the space longer, hired their own caterer and closed the bathrooms to the public.
Other brides contacted by the Tribune weren't afforded such courtesies.
But they should have been offered relaxed rules — for a price. Maybe a high price.
