Costs next year to repair Sewerage and Water Board facilities and to build new ones are estimated at more than $345 million, according to a presentation given Monday on the utility's 2018 capital budget. Of that, more than $83 million is budgeted to go to the utility's drainage department, which has been dogged by power and pump problems exposed during floods over the summer.
More than $56 million worth of projects in the drainage budget have no set funding source, according to Katie Dignan, who oversees infrastructure work completed jointly by the city and the Sewerage and Water Board. Dignan told committee members Monday that past Sewerage and Water Board members had either deferred capital budgets to future years or left drainage allocations unfunded, forcing officials to tap into the utility's water and sewer departments to plug the gap.
"A lot of these are the emergency repairs that are going on right now at the pump stations and the turbines," Dignan said. "But at this point in time, I don't have a revenue source that I can tell you to tap into."
Dignan instead advised board members to convene a meeting in January to discuss the issue. The 2018 capital budget will go to a full board vote on Wednesday.
Dignan suggested about $27 million in funding could come from the agency's bond proceeds, requiring funds to be diverted from the utility's operations and management budget. That budget, which was also presented Monday, is projected to climb by $43 million next year to pay for more staff positions.
Marina Kahn, the utility's newly hired finance and administration head, said the Sewerage and Water Board is budgeted for 1,814 staff positions in 2018, compared with 1,179 positions currently filled. The 2018 tally includes 381 existing vacancies and 254 new positions, a portion of which would be allotted for human resources, communications and billing and field workers such as engineers and pump operators, Marina said.
Ron Spooner, the Sewerage and Water Board's engineering chief, said available funds will be used for "high-priority" projects, but what remains of the $56 million in unfunded projects could languish if not addressed.
In sum, the Sewerage and Water Board's 2018 capital budget earmarks roughly $1.2 billion for 253 total infrastructure projects, including work completed, now under construction or in pre-build stages. About 62 percent, or $764 million, of those projects are currently under construction, according to Monday's presentation.
Drainage projects account for nearly half of the full $1.2 billion in the capital budget, though most of those dollars are tied up in ongoing canal projects led by the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers. Nearly $39 million is being directed toward emergency drainage repairs.
"Will every project be funded at this point in time? No, it will not," Spooner said. "But we do need this capital program to move forward in some fashion so that we can do these projects."
Board committee members on Monday chafed at a slew of costly change-orders that have caused spending on ongoing projects to balloon, in some cases, by more than 60 percent.
Among those projects with soaring costs are emergency repairs to Turbine No. 5 at the Carrollton Plant. That project has racked up nearly $825,000 in addition to the original $1.2 million set in the contract, a nearly 70 percent increase. General Electric is making the repairs.
A separate $6.7 million contract to Boh Brothers Construction Co. to fund water line replacements in the French Quarter and Central Business District had piled on nearly $3.5 million in change orders. The latest change order for that project, brought Tuesday before the committee, totaled more $1 million.
Board members were also critical of two emergency contracts authorized after the summer floods. Both are poised for term extensions that would nearly double their original price tags.
