Having already established himself as one of the 20th-century greats, the artist's latest works ensure his reputation will continue to grow
Thinking big... David Hockney's large-scale Bigger Trees Near Warter oil painting. Photograph: Dan Kitwood/Getty
David Hockney's gift of the most ambitious of his recent landscape paintings to the Tate proves once and for all what a great national treasure this man is.
At a time when art is escalating insanely in market value, he has presented the spectacularly scaled Bigger Trees Near Warter to the museum that embodied modern art in Britain when he was growing up, and still does. But without in the least wanting to diminish this act of generosity, it is surely legitimate to ask how his gift really stands up as a work of art.
Hockney has attracted, in the later part of his career, some vicious and unfair reviews. He's been so famous for so long that critics can't resist a pop. In the words of Anton Ego, the restaurant critic in the film Ratatouille: "The job of a critic is in many ways easy. Negative reviews are fun to write."
I think there really is a difficulty with Hockney's later work, but only in comparison with his beginnings. One of the pleasures of visiting museums around the country is to come across scintillating 1960s Hockneys, like the Walker Art Gallery, Liverpool's 1966 canvas Peter getting out of Nick's pool. In the most liberating decade of the twentieth century Hockney was the most liberated artist.
Still, that was then and this is now. Here is an older, so much older, Hockney driving his car around Yorkshire, smoking, painting. And is it, after all, just the last gasp of an exhausted talent?
It is a joy to report on how good they are. Hockney's big paintings of woodlands in the changing seasons have a bounce that's totally different from anything else in the entire history of British landscape art. It's impossible to detect much influence of Constable or Gainsborough or the melancholy of Ivon Hitchens in these big, bright visions. That's what makes them so fresh and interesting - so young, actually. But perhaps there is something of the Victorians - a pre-Raphaelite glow or even the apocalyptic light of John Martin - in their lurid glimpses of a beauty we tend to forget is still there.
