Things were beginning to look up for the ECC Compton football team.
The Tartars turned two Golden West turnovers – one at the end of the first half, another on the Rustlers�� first drive of the third quarter – into a pair of Anthony Sandoval field goals and trailed by just a point early in the second half.
And then, calamity. Compton turned the ball over on two of its next three possessions and allowed 21 unanswered points in just over four minutes en route to a 45-20 loss at home Saturday afternoon. The Tartars fell to 3-4 overall and 2-2 in the SCFA American Division Mountain Conference.
��It just got away from us (in the third quarter),�� said Compton coach Sean Fernandes after he watched Cypress High graduate Michael Vanderipe throw a pair of long touchdown passes to Brandon Grissom in the third. He also watched as Sheldon Dennis let a backward pass fall to the ground and Golden West linebacker Preston Margain scooped it up for a 26-yard score. ��The more disciplined team won today.��
The Rustlers (4-3, 3-1 Mountain Conference) led, 14-0, midway through the first quarter on a 1-yard run by Tim Atencio and a 7-yard pass from Vanderipe to Raphael Enriquez.
But the Tartars came back with 13 quick points of their own. Donyae Coleman caught a 9-yard TD pass from Mark Slorp with 59 seconds left in the half and Compton recovered a Golden West fumble at the Ruslter 46 with 25 seconds remaining. Slorp (9-16-1, 78 yards) moved Compton to the Golden West 3 and Sandoval booted a 21-yard field goal as time expired.
The Rustlers received the kickoff to open the third but turned the ball over just two plays later when Branden Warner sacked Vanderipe, forced a fumble and recovered the ball at the Golden West 22. Three plays later, Sandoval kicked a 38-yard field goal to make the score 14-13.
That, however, is when things went south for Compton. Vanderipe (14-24-2, 254 yards, 4 TDs) found Grissom behind the Tartar secondary for a 36-yard score with 8:57 left in the third to build the lead to 21-13. On Golden West��s next drive, Vanderipe and Grissom hooked up again, this time for 77 yards and a 28-13 advantage. Compton��s next drive ended when Margain recovered the backward pass to Dennis, and the rout was on.
Grissom added a 33-yard TD pass in the fourth and finished with 191 yards on six receptions. Coleman caught five passes for 63 yards and Steve Tirogene had five receptions for 62 yards, including a 20-yard TD from Jeremy Edwards in the fourth.
– David Felton
